---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "@components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import CaseStudyMeta from "@components/CaseStudyMeta.astro";
import Figure from "@components/Figure.astro";
import ProcessColumns from "@components/ProcessColumns.astro";
import Pivot from "@components/Pivot.astro";
import Strip from "@components/Strip.astro";
import Metrics from "@components/Metrics.astro";
import NextCaseStudy from "@components/NextCaseStudy.astro";

import ibm01 from "@img/ibm-01.webp";
import ibm02 from "@img/ibm-02.webp";
import ibm03 from "@img/ibm-03.webp";
import ibm05 from "@img/ibm-05.svg";
import ibm06 from "@img/ibm-06.svg";
import ibm07 from "@img/ibm-07.svg";
import ibm08 from "@img/ibm-08.webp";
import ibm09 from "@img/ibm-09.webp";
import ibm10 from "@img/ibm-10.webp";

const title = "Drawing less of the map for the questions banks ask every day";
const description =
	"IBM's data lineage tool was built for the audit. A redesign that made a folded view the everyday default and kept the full map one click away.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<Fragment slot="hero">
	<CaseStudyHero eyebrow={["Product design", "IBM", "Data lineage in watsonx"]} title="Drawing less of the map for the questions banks ask every day">
		IBM's data lineage tool was built for the audit: draw every step of a bank's data pipeline, however long it takes. But more and more people were opening it for quick, everyday questions, and waiting hours for an answer. I chose to show almost nothing by default, kept the full audit view one click away, and built the model so other teams could use it too.
	</CaseStudyHero>

	<CaseStudyMeta
		items={[
			{ label: "Role", value: "Design lead, three-person UX team" },
			{ label: "Partners", value: "ING, State Street, Rabobank" },
			{ label: "Timeline", value: "9 months" },
			{ label: "Research base", value: "20+ interviews, co-creation workshops every two weeks with three client banks" },
		]}
	/>
	</Fragment>

	<Figure
		video={{
			poster: "/video/data-lineage-tour-poster.webp",
			sources: [
				{ src: "/video/data-lineage-tour-720.mp4", media: "(max-width: 700px)" },
				{ src: "/video/data-lineage-tour-1440.mp4" },
			],
		}}
		alt="The Welcome to Data Lineage tour in IBM watsonx, stepping through three panels: trust your data, investigate your data pipeline, and analyze and share valuable insights, each with a diagram of the lineage view behind it"
		figNum="Fig. 01"
		lead="What shipped, in three steps."
		wide
		fit="contain"
		deck
		deckLabel="What shipped"
	>
		IBM's lineage product still opens by saying what it's for: trust your data, investigate the pipeline,
		analyze what you find. Behind those three lines sit nine months, three client banks, and a model the ETL
		and AI teams went on to adapt for their own data.
	</Figure>

	<Figure
		src={ibm01}
		alt="IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog, Lineage tab for SAVINGS_ACCOUNTS. The starting asset sits in the centre with its business terms and 2 masked columns; three original-source CSV files sit on the left grouped as Client 360 and Customer 360 with counts like 2 of 24 assets; two reports and a model marked Final target sit on the right, each showing business terms and column counts."
		figNum="Fig. 02"
		lead="The everyday view."
		wide
		fit="contain"
		deck
		deckLabel="Shipped"
	>
		Lineage opens on the asset in question, its sources on the left and final targets on the right, with everything between folded into counts like "2 of 24 assets" until someone asks.
	</Figure>

	<section id="audit">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>A tool built only for the audit</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>Data lineage is how a bank proves where a number came from. Regulators expect it, and getting it wrong can cost up to $23 million in fines. IBM Infosphere had drawn that map for a decade by rendering every one of a pipeline's three hundred possible failure points, which meant millions of queries before anything appeared.</p>
		<Figure
			src={ibm02}
			alt="IBM Infosphere Information Governance Catalog, Data Lineage for EFF_DURATION: an inventory panel listing 281 assets on the left and, on the right, a dense diagram of hundreds of tiny nodes joined by grey lines, too small to read"
			figNum="Fig. 03"
			lead="Built for the audit, used for everything."
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Infosphere"
		>
			A small lineage by Infosphere's standards still drew 281 assets, every node too small to read, before anyone could ask a simple question.
		</Figure>
		<p><strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-foot="IBM Infosphere, before the redesign|Up to $23M in compliance fines at stake">That thoroughness made sense for an audit.</strong> It made no sense for the growing number of people who opened lineage for a quick answer about where one number came from, then waited hours and clicked more than a hundred times to get it.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="everyday">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>Designing for the everyday question</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>ING's Chief Data Officer described the ideal as seeing the whole factory floor, "where everything is and where things are going wrong." That vision was real, and audits depend on it. But in co-creation sessions with the three client banks every two weeks, everyday users kept asking for something narrower: the first useful answer, then the option to keep going. In desirability testing, "show me the headline, then let me drill in" beat "show me everything."</p>
		<Figure
			src={ibm03}
			alt="Persona and experience map for Betty, a business analyst who consumes data. The top block lists her asks, need statements, negative emotions, pain points, and requested metadata. Below, an emotional curve runs through five phases, naive use of data, guided use, development of autonomy, continuing collaboration, and mastery, with the lowest points where she cannot answer why her data is trustworthy and realizes she depends on data producers."
			figNum="Fig. 04"
			lead="Two sides, one map."
			wide
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Journey map"
		>
			Betty, a business analyst, hits her lowest point when she can't say why her data is trustworthy. Mastery, at the far right, means moving between the business and technical sides herself.
		</Figure>
		<p>None of the existing references solved it. A competitor, a patent filed during the project, and another IBM team each handled one part of the problem and broke on another.</p>
		<ProcessColumns
			land
			deckLabel="References"
			deckCaption="Each reference solved one part of the problem and broke on another."
			items={[
				{
					title: "The competitor",
					imgSrc: ibm05,
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Competitor: one pink data lineage node joined by a dashed line to a grey hidden node with a single button, Show next node",
					body: "Fast, one step at a time, and dozens of clicks to reach a distant source.",
				},
				{
					title: "The patent",
					imgSrc: ibm06,
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Patent: four nodes in a row, the second greyed out as a hidden node, with an arrow skipping over it from the first node to the third",
					body: "Hid steps cleanly, but only ones someone had flagged by hand.",
				},
				{
					title: "The neighbor",
					imgSrc: ibm07,
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Internal: two pink nodes with two grey hidden nodes between them, covered by one dark button reading Show 2 hidden nodes",
					body: "Grouped nodes invited exploring, but the counts cost the same slow queries.",
				},
			]}
		/>
		<p style="margin-top:40px">Showing less carried a real risk. An audit needs everything a piece of bad data touched, and a view that folds steps away can look like it's hiding the problem. But drawing everything was exactly what made the old tool slow and expensive. So the design kept both. The folded view became the default for everyday questions, and one button draws the full map for an audit, with a message saying it will take time and roughly how long. <strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-label="Both views" data-deck-foot="The audit view, kept one click away|IBM watsonx">The factory floor was still there. It just wasn't the first thing anyone had to wait for.</strong></p>

		<Pivot label="The call" heading="Show the source and the consumer. Draw nothing else until someone asks.">
			The Summary View opens on the first source and the final consumer, with everything between them folded into counts like "2 of 24 assets." That's the business lineage, a quick look for stakeholders who need the high-level story. The technical lineage sits beneath it, reached through filters, for people doing deep investigation: expand a step, filter to poor quality scores or ownership, or narrow the view to a single column out of thousands. The concept won testing on both desirability and usability the first time out, and the time to a useful answer went from hours to seconds.
		</Pivot>

		<Strip
			full
			solo
			deckLabel="Summary View"
			figNum="Fig. 05"
			lead="From a quick look to deep investigation."
			items={[
				{
					frameN: "01",
					dataLabel: "Land on source and consumer",
					imgSrc: ibm08,
					imgAlt: "IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog Lineage view for SAVINGS_ACCOUNTS, with an annotation reading: assets at either end of a lineage do not change often so it's safe to cache them, assets in the middle can change by the hour which means frequent calls to the back end for updates",
				},
				{
					frameN: "02",
					dataLabel: "Expand on demand",
					imgSrc: ibm09,
					imgAlt: "Annotated screen titled Lineage Navigation, subtitled you should be able to explore lineage like you were using Google Maps: hovering an asset reveals a directional arrow, clicking it opens a menu with Show next asset, Show assets to, and Show all",
				},
				{
					frameN: "03",
					dataLabel: "Filter to what matters",
					imgSrc: ibm10,
					imgAlt: "Annotated screen of the COLLECTIONS_DW node showing 2 of 14 columns filtered into view, each with its own business terms, and a note that deeper technical, operational and governance metadata sits in a toggleable info panel",
				},
			]}
		>
			Land on source and consumer, expand on demand, then filter down to the columns that matter. The diagram never gets bigger than the question.
		</Strip>
	</section>

	<section id="mandate">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Designing past the mandate</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>The mandate was lineage for this one project. When the ETL team and the AI model team came asking for the same logic, for pipeline runs and training data that looked nothing like banking transactions, I saw that their needs weren't all that different from ours. <strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-label="One model" data-deck-foot="Generalizing the lineage model|IBM watsonx">Generalizing the model risked the ship date. Staying narrow risked several teams quietly rebuilding the same thing.</strong></p>
		<p>I recommended generalizing, and it wasn't a hard sell. Design leadership was glad to see the project contribute new patterns to the whole IBM design community, and the design systems team suggested changes so the patterns fit the wider system. The model generalized, and the original timeline held.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="outcome">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>What changed</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>Measured after launch, and in the years since:</p>
		<Metrics
			items={[
				{ n: "−90%", label: "Loading time, at the high end, from hours to seconds" },
				{ n: "Red Dot", label: "Award for the redesign" },
			]}
		/>
		<p>Six years later, IBM still ships the same patterns, and the ETL and AI model teams adapted the model rather than building their own.</p>
		<Figure
			video={{
				poster: "/video/lineage-six-years-on-poster.webp",
				sources: [
					{ src: "/video/lineage-six-years-on-720.mp4", media: "(max-width: 700px)" },
					{ src: "/video/lineage-six-years-on-1440.mp4" },
				],
			}}
			alt="IBM Cloud Pak for Data, Lineage tab for the gdpr-analysis-workbook report: opens on two original-source assets feeding a starting asset, then opens the Show query items panel to pick which columns appear in the lineage graph"
			figNum="Fig. 06"
			lead="Still the front door, six years later."
			tall
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Six years on"
		>
			The work outlived the team that built it. Open IBM's lineage today and it still opens folded to
			source and consumer, still expands a step at a time, and still asks which columns are worth showing
			before it draws them.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="reflection">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>Looking back</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>The call I was least sure of was how to move between levels of detail. A quick business view for stakeholders and deep technical detective work serve very different people, and a single dataset could carry thousands of columns, tags, and other metadata. Getting the step from one to the other right took a lot of research and many rounds of iteration.</p>
		<p>The hardest part to manage wasn't the design, though. It was keeping seven teams aligned on decisions they had already made, and I spent hours re-litigating choices people had agreed to a month earlier. Since then, every project I work on has a shared decision diary from the first week, recording what we decided, when, and why.</p>
	</section>

	<NextCaseStudy slot="close" href="/case-studies/hpe-ai-troubleshooting-agent" label="Selling the big idea, shipping the smallest one" />
</CaseStudyLayout>
